[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding JPA Inheritance Mapping Strategies"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Object-oriented programming languages like Java support inheritance, allowing classes to inherit fields and methods from parent classes. Relational databases, however, do not natively support object-oriented inheritance. JPA provides three primary strategies to bridge this gap: Single Table, Joined, and Table per Class."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Single Table Strategy (InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Single Table strategy maps an entire inheritance hierarchy to a single database table. A discriminator column is used to distinguish between the different types of entities stored in the table. This is the default strategy in JPA and generally offers the best performance because polymorphism requires no SQL joins."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
@DiscriminatorColumn(name = "vehicle_type", discriminatorType = DiscriminatorType.STRING)
public abstract class Vehicle {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private String manufacturer;
}"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@DiscriminatorValue("CAR")
public class Car extends Vehicle {
    private int seatCount;
}"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Joined Strategy (InheritanceType.JOINED)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the Joined strategy, each class in the hierarchy is represented by its own database table. The parent class table contains columns common to all entities, while subclass tables contain only the fields specific to that subclass, linked via a foreign key constraint to the parent table."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.JOINED)
public abstract class Payment {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private BigDecimal amount;
}"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@PrimaryKeyJoinColumn(name = "payment_id")
public class CreditCardPayment extends Payment {
    private String cardNumber;
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Table per Class Strategy (InheritanceType.TABLE_PER_CLASS)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Table per Class strategy maps each concrete class in the entity hierarchy to its own distinct database table. The table for a concrete subclass contains columns for all inherited properties plus all properties defined locally in the subclass. This strategy often requires SQL UNION queries for polymorphic queries."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.TABLE_PER_CLASS)
public abstract class Employee {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.TABLE)
    private Long id;
    private String name;
}"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
public class Manager extends Employee {
    private double bonus;
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparison and Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Single Table: Best performance, simple queries, but can result in many null columns for subclass-specific fields."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Joined: Normalized database schema and eliminates null columns, but requires expensive JOIN operations during queries."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Table per Class: Good for independent subclass operations, but polymorphic queries can severely degrade performance due to UNION operations."}]
